If we find the left eigenvectors of such a matrix, then its inverse (because it projects onto a plane with dimensionn less than that of the matrix itself) will produce the normal vector.  To find the left eigenvectors, we take the matrix in the form $I - AA^T$ since that equals the 0 matrix exactly when $AA^T =  I,$ which is true when the columns of $A$ are orthonormal (which is precisely what an orthonormal matrix must be).
So in our case, we want to find the eigenvectors that correspond to the zero eigenvalue of the matrix
\[\begin{pmatrix} \frac{13}{14} & -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{3}{14}
\\ -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{5}{7} & \frac{3}{7}
\\ \frac{3}{14} & \frac{3}{7} & \frac{5}{14} \end{pmatrix}
- \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0
\\ 0 & 1 & 0
\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{1}{14} & -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{3}{14}
\\ -\frac{1}{7} & -\frac{2}{7} & \frac{3}{7}
\\ \frac{3}{14} & \frac{3}{7} & -\frac{9}{14} \end{pmatrix}.
\]We see, from here, that $(-1,-4,3)$ is an eigenvector of this matrix, which means that, in the matrix's row space, (-1,-4,3) is also an eigenvector, so vector itself should be the normal vector if we took it as a instance of a column vector.  Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}}.$ </think> <answer> \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} </answer>

